skill: Manual Testing

@ Testing Fundamentals | B
What is the main purpose of software testing?
= To find defects and check that the software meets its requirements
- To prove that the software has no defects at all
- To write the production code
- To design the database
? Testing provides information about quality. It cannot prove that no defects exist.

@ Testing Fundamentals | B
What is a defect?
= A flaw where the actual behavior differs from the expected behavior
- A requirement written in a document
- A test case that passed
- A feature request from a user
? A defect (bug) is a deviation from the expected result.

@ Testing Fundamentals | I
What is the difference between verification and validation?
= Verification checks that we are building the product right, validation checks that we are building the right product
- Verification is done only by users, validation only by developers
- They mean exactly the same thing
- Verification runs the software, validation only reads emails
? Verification includes reviews and inspections against specifications. Validation checks the product against user needs, usually by executing it.

@ Testing Fundamentals | I
Why is exhaustive testing considered impossible for most software?
= The number of possible inputs and paths is practically infinite
- Testers are not allowed to test everything
- Software never changes
- Test tools are too expensive
? Because everything cannot be tested, teams use risk-based prioritization and test design techniques.

@ Testing Fundamentals | I
A developer makes a mistake while writing code. What is this mistake called, and what can it lead to?
= An error, which can introduce a defect that may cause a failure
- A failure, which always introduces an error
- A test case, which causes a defect
- A requirement, which causes a failure
? Terminology chain: a human error leads to a defect in the code, which may cause a failure when executed.

@ Test Cases and Scenarios | B
Which set of elements best describes a complete test case?
= ID, preconditions, steps, test data and expected result
- Only a title
- Only the bug id
- Only the source code
? A good test case lets anyone execute it and decide clearly whether it passed.

@ Test Cases and Scenarios | I
What is a test scenario?
= A high-level description of a functionality to be tested, from which test cases are derived
- A detailed script with exact click coordinates
- A defect report
- A project schedule
? One scenario, such as verifying login, may produce many test cases.

@ Test Cases and Scenarios | I
Which practice makes test cases better?
= Make them clear, repeatable and independent, each with an expected result
- Combine as many checks as possible into one case
- Leave out expected results to save time
- Write them only after the release
? Independent, well-defined cases are easier to run, maintain and debug.

@ Test Cases and Scenarios | I
What does a requirements traceability matrix show?
= The mapping between requirements and test cases, to verify coverage
- The list of employees on the project
- The bug count per developer
- The server configuration
? Traceability helps confirm every requirement has at least one test and shows the impact of changes.

@ Test Cases and Scenarios | I
For a login page, which is a negative test case?
= Enter a valid username with a wrong password and verify an error message is shown
- Enter valid credentials and verify the dashboard opens
- Verify the page title is correct
- Verify the logo is visible
? Negative tests check how the system handles invalid input or unexpected conditions.

@ Test Design Techniques | I
A field accepts ages from 18 to 60. Which values does boundary value analysis focus on?
= 17, 18, 19 and 59, 60, 61
- 1, 2, 3 only
- 30 only
- Random values between 0 and 100
? Defects often occur at the edges, so tests target values just below, on and just above each boundary.

@ Test Design Techniques | I
What is equivalence partitioning?
= Dividing inputs into groups expected to behave the same and testing one value from each group
- Testing every possible input
- Running the same test many times
- Splitting the team into two groups
? It reduces the number of test cases while keeping coverage of the different behaviors.

@ Test Design Techniques | A
A field accepts whole numbers from 1 to 100. How many partitions does equivalence partitioning identify at minimum?
= 3 (below 1, 1 to 100, above 100)
- 1
- 100
- 2
? One valid partition and two invalid partitions (too low and too high) give three groups.

@ Test Design Techniques | I
Which technique represents combinations of conditions and their resulting actions in a table?
= Decision table testing
- Smoke testing
- Load testing
- Exploratory testing
? Decision tables are useful for complex business rules with several conditions.

@ Bug Life Cycle | B
What is the usual first status of a newly reported bug?
= New
- Closed
- Reopened
- Deferred
? A bug starts as New and is then assigned, fixed, retested and closed.

@ Bug Life Cycle | I
After a developer fixes a bug, which status normally comes before the tester retests it?
= Fixed
- New
- Rejected
- Duplicate
? The developer marks the bug Fixed, and then the tester verifies the fix and moves it to Closed or Reopened.

@ Bug Life Cycle | I
What does the Reopened status mean?
= The bug still reproduces after the fix and goes back to the developer
- The bug was reported twice
- The bug was postponed to a later release
- The bug was rejected as invalid
? If retesting shows the problem remains, the tester reopens the bug.

@ Bug Life Cycle | I
What is the difference between severity and priority?
= Severity is the impact of the defect on the system, priority is the urgency of fixing it
- Severity is how soon it will be fixed, priority is the technical impact
- They are always the same
- Severity is set by the customer, priority by the compiler
? A crash in a rarely used feature may be high severity but low priority.

@ Bug Life Cycle | A
A spelling mistake appears in the company name on the home page. Which severity and priority are most likely?
= Low severity and high priority
- High severity and low priority
- High severity and high priority
- Low severity and low priority
? The functionality is not affected, but the visible branding error needs a quick fix.

@ Bug Life Cycle | I
What does the Deferred status mean for a bug?
= The fix is postponed to a later release
- The bug is fixed and verified
- The bug is a duplicate
- The bug cannot be reproduced
? Deferred bugs are valid but are scheduled for a future version.

@ SDLC and STLC | B
Which SDLC model is linear, with each phase completed before the next begins?
= Waterfall
- Agile
- Spiral
- Prototype
? Waterfall moves through requirements, design, implementation, testing and maintenance in sequence.

@ SDLC and STLC | B
In which STLC phase are test cases usually written?
= Test case development (design)
- Test closure
- Deployment
- Requirement gathering by the customer
? After requirement analysis and test planning, test cases and test data are prepared.

@ SDLC and STLC | I
What is the central idea of the V-model?
= Every development phase has a corresponding testing phase
- Testing starts only after release
- Development and testing never overlap
- Testing is optional
? The left side of the V is development, the right side is verification, and each level is linked.

@ SDLC and STLC | I
Which is the correct order of the main STLC phases?
= Requirement analysis, test planning, test case development, environment setup, test execution, test closure
- Test execution, test planning, requirement analysis, test closure
- Test closure, test planning, test execution, requirement analysis
- Environment setup, test closure, test execution, test planning
? STLC starts by understanding requirements and ends with reporting and closing the testing cycle.

@ SDLC and STLC | I
What is an entry criterion in testing?
= A condition that must be met before a testing phase can begin
- A bug that blocks the release
- The last test case of a suite
- A report written after release
? Examples include a stable build, a ready test environment and approved test cases.

@ SDLC and STLC | I
How is testing performed in Agile teams?
= Continuously and collaboratively within each sprint
- Only at the end of the project
- Only by a separate team after release
- Only through automation
? Testers work with developers and product owners throughout every iteration.

@ Types of Testing | B
Which testing checks that new changes have not broken existing functionality?
= Regression testing
- Smoke testing
- Usability testing
- Load testing
? Regression tests are re-run after changes to confirm earlier behavior still works.

@ Types of Testing | I
What is smoke testing?
= A quick check of critical functions to decide whether a build is stable enough for deeper testing
- A detailed test of every feature
- Testing only performance under load
- Testing by the end user at the client site
? If the smoke test fails, the build is returned to the developers.

@ Types of Testing | I
What is User Acceptance Testing (UAT)?
= Testing by end users or clients to confirm the software meets their business needs
- Testing a single method by a developer
- Testing the performance of the network
- Testing the installation scripts only
? UAT is usually the final validation before the software is accepted for release.

@ Types of Testing | B
Which type of testing is performed without knowledge of the internal code?
= Black-box testing
- White-box testing
- Unit testing with code coverage
- Code review
? Black-box testers validate inputs and outputs against requirements.

@ Types of Testing | I
What is exploratory testing?
= Simultaneous learning, test design and execution without fully scripted test cases
- Running only automated scripts
- Testing after all bugs are fixed
- Reviewing documents without using the software
? The tester explores the application, using experience to find defects scripts might miss.
